    "pressure": {
      "file": "gland_packing_assembly_pressure.glb",
      "title": "Pressure is broken down along the packing",
      "description": "Packing seals by throttling: the gland compresses the rings so they press radially on the sleeve, and leakage flows along a long, narrow path, losing pressure ring by ring. Most of the pressure drop happens in the rings nearest the gland, so those wear first. A packed gland must leak a little (≈ 40–60 drops per minute) — the liquid lubricates and removes friction heat; over-tightening burns the packing and grooves the sleeve. The lantern ring feeds clean flush liquid into the middle of the set.",
      "render": "normal",
      "annotations": []
    }
  },
